# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
EN = {}
EN[169] = "The girls were a little surprised at not finding one sack. <<OKSANA>>“Nothing to be done, this one will do for us,”<<N>> babbled Oksana. All took hold of the sack and heaved it onto the sledge. The Headman resolved to keep silent, reasoning that if he shouted to be let out and the sack untied, the foolish girls would run away and think that the devil was sitting in the sack, and he would be left in the street, perhaps till tomorrow. The girls meanwhile, joining hands in a friendly way, flew like a whirlwind with the sledge over the creaking snow. A crowd of them, playing about, sat on the sledge; others climbed onto the Headman himself. The Headman resolved to bear everything. At last they arrived, threw wide the door in the porch and the cottage, and with laughter dragged in the sack. <<GIRLS>>“Let us see what is lying here,”<<N>> they all cried, rushing to untie it. Then the hiccups, which had not ceased to torment the Headman the whole time he sat in the sack, grew so strong that he began to hiccup and cough at the top of his voice. <<GIRLS>>“Oh, there is someone sitting in there!”<<N>> they all cried, and in fright rushed out of the door."
EN[170] = "<<CHUB>>“What the devil! Where are you rushing off to, like mad things?”<<N>> said Chub, coming in at the door."
EN[171] = "<<OKSANA>>“Oh, father!”<<N>> said Oksana. <<OKSANA>>“There is someone sitting in the sack!”"
EN[172] = "<<CHUB>>“In the sack? Where did you get this sack?”"
EN[173] = "<<GIRLS>>“The blacksmith threw it down in the middle of the road,”<<N>> they all said at once."
EN[174] = "<<CHUB>>“Well, so, did I not say...?”<<N>> thought Chub to himself. <<CHUB>>“What are you frightened of? Let us see: come now, my good man, I beg you not to be angry that we do not call you by name and patronymic, climb out of the sack!”"
EN[175] = "The Headman climbed out."
EN[176] = "<<GIRLS>>“Ah!”<<N>> cried the girls."
EN[177] = "<<CHUB>>“And the Headman has got in there too,”<<N>> Chub said to himself in bewilderment, measuring him from head to foot. <<CHUB>>“Look at that!... Eh!...”<<N>> More than this he could not say."
EN[178] = "The Headman himself was no less embarrassed and did not know what to begin. <<HEAD>>“It must be cold outside?”<<N>> he said, turning to Chub."
EN[179] = "<<CHUB>>“There is a bit of frost,”<<N>> answered Chub, <<CHUB>>“and allow me to ask you, what do you grease your boots with, lard or tar?”<<N>> He did not mean to say this; he meant to ask: how did you, Headman, get into this sack; but he himself did not understand how he had said something quite different."
EN[180] = "<<HEAD>>“Tar is better,”<<N>> said the Headman. <<HEAD>>“Well, farewell, Chub!”<<N>> And, pulling his cap down, he left the cottage."
EN[181] = "<<CHUB>>“Why did I ask, like a fool, what he greases his boots with!”<<N>> said Chub, glancing at the door by which the Headman had gone out. <<CHUB>>“Good for Solokha! To stuff a man like that into a sack!... Look at her, the devil's woman! And I am a fool... but where is that accursed sack?”"
EN[182] = "<<OKSANA>>“I threw it in the corner, there is nothing more in it,”<<N>> said Oksana."
EN[183] = "<<CHUB>>“I know these tricks, nothing in it! Hand it here: there is another one sitting in there! Shake it well... What, no?... Look at her, the accursed woman! And to look at her, a saint, as if she had never put anything but Lenten food in her mouth.”"
EN[184] = "But let us leave Chub to pour out his vexation at leisure, and return to the blacksmith, for it must surely be nine o'clock outside by now."
EN[186] = "At first it seemed terrifying to Vakula when he rose from the earth to such a height that he could see nothing below, and flew like a fly right under the moon, so that if he had not bent down a little he would have caught it with his cap. But a little later he took heart and began to make fun of the devil. It amused him beyond measure how the devil sneezed and coughed when he took the cypress-wood cross from his neck and held it out to him. On purpose he would lift his hand to scratch his head, and the devil, thinking he was about to be crossed, flew still faster. Everything was bright up on high. The air, in a light silver mist, was transparent. Everything could be seen, and one could even notice how a sorcerer swept past them like a whirlwind, sitting in a pot; how the stars, gathered in a heap, played blind man's buff; how a whole swarm of spirits swirled to one side like a cloud; how a devil, dancing in the moonlight, took off his cap when he saw the blacksmith riding along on horseback; how a broom came flying back, on which a witch had evidently just ridden where she was needed... they met much more such rubbish. All of them, seeing the blacksmith, stopped for a minute to look at him and then rushed on again and went about their business; the blacksmith flew on, and suddenly Petersburg blazed up before him, all in fire. (There was an illumination at the time, for some occasion.) The devil, flying over the barrier, turned into a horse, and the blacksmith saw himself on a spirited trotter in the middle of the street. My God! Clatter, thunder, glitter; on both sides four-storey walls loomed; the horse's hooves and the sound of the wheels echoed like thunder and rang back from four sides; houses grew and seemed to rise out of the earth at every step; bridges trembled; carriages flew; cabmen and postilions shouted; the snow whistled under a thousand sledges flying from every side; pedestrians huddled and crowded under houses strung with lamps, and their enormous shadows flickered along the walls, reaching the chimneys and roofs with their heads. In amazement the blacksmith looked around on all sides. It seemed to him that all the houses had fixed on him their countless fiery eyes and were watching. Gentlemen in cloth-covered fur coats he saw so many that he did not know to whom to take off his cap. <<VAKULA>>“My God, how many gentry there are here!”<<N>> thought the blacksmith. <<VAKULA>>“I think everyone who walks down the street in a fur coat is an assessor, an assessor! And those who ride in such wonderful glass-windowed carriages, if they are not mayors, then surely commissaries, and perhaps even more.”<<N>> His words were interrupted by the devil's question: <<DEVIL>>“Shall we go straight to the Empress?”<<N>> <<VAKULA>>“No, it is frightening,”<<N>> thought the blacksmith. <<VAKULA>>“Somewhere here, I don't know where, the Zaporozhians who passed through Dikanka in the autumn are lodging. They were travelling from the Sich with papers for the Empress; all the same I should consult them. Hey, Satan, climb into my pocket, and lead me to the Zaporozhians!”<<N>> In one minute the devil grew thin and became so small that he climbed into his pocket without difficulty. And Vakula had no time to look round before he found himself in front of a big house, went, not knowing how, up the stairs, opened the door, and drew back a little from the glitter at the sight of a splendidly furnished room; but he took heart a little, recognising the very Zaporozhians who had passed through Dikanka, sitting on silk sofas, with their tarred boots tucked under them, smoking the strongest tobacco, usually called roots."
EN[187] = "<<VAKULA>>“Good health to you, gentlemen! God help you! So this is where we meet!”<<N>> said the blacksmith, coming close and bowing to the ground."
EN[188] = "<<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“What man is that?”<<N>> asked one who sat right in front of the blacksmith, of another who sat farther off."
EN[189] = "<<VAKULA>>“And you do not recognise me?”<<N>> said the blacksmith. <<VAKULA>>“It is I, Vakula the blacksmith! When you passed through Dikanka in the autumn, you stayed, God give you every health and long life, nearly two days. And I put a new tire on the front wheel of your wagon then!”"
EN[190] = "<<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“Ah!”<<N>> said the same Zaporozhian. <<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“It is that blacksmith who paints so grandly. Hello, countryman! What has God brought you here for?”"
EN[191] = "<<VAKULA>>“Oh, just wanted to have a look, they say...”"
EN[192] = "<<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“Well, countryman,”<<N>> said the Zaporozhian, drawing himself up and wishing to show that he too could speak in Russian. <<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“Vot, a beeg city?”"
EN[193] = "The blacksmith did not want to disgrace himself and appear a novice either; besides, as we have had occasion to see above, he knew the learned language himself. <<VAKULA>>“A notable province!”<<N>> he answered indifferently. <<VAKULA>>“Nothing to say, the houses are enormous, important paintings hang everywhere. Many houses are inscribed with letters of gold leaf, to the extreme. Nothing to say, a marvellous proportion!”"
EN[194] = "The Zaporozhians, hearing the blacksmith express himself so freely, drew a conclusion very advantageous to him."
EN[195] = "<<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“We will talk with you more later, countryman; now we are going at once to the Empress.”"
EN[196] = "<<VAKULA>>“To the Empress? Be so kind, gentlemen, take me with you!”"
EN[197] = "<<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“You?”<<N>> said the Zaporozhian, with the look with which a nurse speaks to her four-year-old charge who asks to be put on a real, big horse. <<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“What will you do there? No, it is not possible.”<<N>> At this a significant expression appeared on his face. <<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“We, brother, shall be talking with the Empress about our own affairs.”"
EN[198] = "<<VAKULA>>“Take me!”<<N>> insisted the blacksmith. <<VAKULA>>“Ask!”<<N>> he whispered quietly to the devil, striking his pocket with his fist. He had hardly said this when another Zaporozhian said: <<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“Let us take him, really, brothers!”"
EN[199] = "<<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“Let us take him, then!”<<N>> said the others."
EN[200] = "<<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“Put on clothes like ours.”"
EN[201] = "The blacksmith hastened to pull on a green coat, when suddenly the door opened and a man with gold lace came in and said it was time to go."
EN[202] = "It seemed strange to the blacksmith again when he rushed along in an enormous carriage, swaying on its springs, when on both sides four-storey houses ran backwards past him and the pavement, thundering, seemed to roll itself under the horses' feet."
EN[203] = "<<VAKULA>>“My God, what a light!”<<N>> thought the blacksmith to himself. <<VAKULA>>“With us it is never so bright by day.”"
EN[204] = "The carriages stopped in front of a palace. The Zaporozhians got out, entered a magnificent hall, and began to climb a brilliantly lit staircase."
EN[205] = "<<VAKULA>>“What a staircase!”<<N>> whispered the blacksmith to himself. <<VAKULA>>“It is a pity to tread on it with one's feet. What decorations! They say fairy tales lie; what the devil do they lie! My God, what banisters! What workmanship! Fifty roubles' worth of iron alone went into this!”"
EN[206] = "Having climbed the stairs, the Zaporozhians passed through the first hall. The blacksmith followed them timidly, fearing at every step to slip on the parquet. They passed through three halls, and the blacksmith did not cease to marvel. Entering the fourth, he involuntarily went up to a picture that hung on the wall. It was the Most Pure Virgin with the Child in her arms. <<VAKULA>>“What a picture! What wonderful painting!”<<N>> he reflected. <<VAKULA>>“Look, it seems to speak! It seems alive! And the Holy Child! And he has pressed his little hands! And smiles, poor thing! And the colours! My God, what colours! There is not a kopeck's worth of ochre here, I think, all verdigris and crimson. And the blue fairly burns! A grand piece of work! The ground must have been laid with white lead. But however wonderful these paintings are, this copper handle,”<<N>> he went on, going up to the door and feeling the lock, <<VAKULA>>“deserves still greater wonder. What clean workmanship! All this, I think, was made by German blacksmiths at the dearest prices...”<<N>> Perhaps the blacksmith would have gone on reasoning a long time if a footman with braid had not jogged his arm and reminded him not to fall behind the others. The Zaporozhians passed through two more halls and stopped. Here they were told to wait. In the hall a number of generals crowded, in gold-embroidered uniforms. The Zaporozhians bowed on all sides and stood in a heap. A minute later there came in, accompanied by a whole retinue, a rather stout man of majestic height, in a hetman's uniform, in yellow boots. His hair was dishevelled, one eye slightly squinting, on his face there was a kind of haughty grandeur, and in all his movements the habit of command could be seen. All the generals, who had been strolling about quite proudly in their gold uniforms, bustled and, with low bows, seemed to catch his every word and even his slightest movement, ready to fly at once to carry it out. But the hetman did not even pay attention, barely nodded his head and went up to the Zaporozhians."
EN[207] = "The Zaporozhians all bowed to his feet."
EN[208] = "<<POTEMKIN>>“Are you all here?”<<N>> he asked, drawling, pronouncing the words a little through his nose."
EN[209] = "<<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“All here, father!”<<N>> answered the Zaporozhians, bowing again."
EN[210] = "<<POTEMKIN>>“You will not forget to speak as I taught you?”"
EN[211] = "<<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“No, father, we will not forget.”"
EN[212] = "<<VAKULA>>“Is that the Tsar?”<<N>> the blacksmith asked one of the Zaporozhians."
EN[213] = "<<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“The Tsar, indeed! That is Potemkin himself,”<<N>> answered the man."
EN[214] = "Voices were heard in another room, and the blacksmith did not know where to put his eyes at the multitude of ladies who came in, in satin dresses with long trains, and courtiers in gold-embroidered coats with bunches of hair at the back. He saw only glitter and nothing more. The Zaporozhians suddenly all fell to the ground and cried with one voice: <<ZAPOROZHIAN>>“Have mercy, Mother! Have mercy!”<<N>> The blacksmith, seeing nothing, stretched himself out on the floor too, with all diligence."
